# =============================================================================
# Set these before the stack will start. `docker compose up` refuses to run
# while any of them is empty. README.md in this folder says what each one is
# and where to get it.
# =============================================================================
VITE_OAUTH_CONFIG=
VITE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
VITE_OAUTH_AUDIENCE=
VITE_OAUTH_SCOPE=
PGRST_JWT_AUD=

# Defaults that differ from the bundled stack. Uncomment to change one.
# JWKS_URL=
# DOCS_API_ROUTE=/rest
# PGRST_JWT_ROLE_CLAIM_KEY=.roles[0]
# VITE_BACKEND_TYPE=custom
# VITE_UI_CUSTOMIZER={"user":{"menu":[{"title":"Microsoft account","url":"https://myaccount.microsoft.com/","target":"newtab"}]}}

# Copy to .env and edit. Used by docker-compose.yml (the Semantius stack).
#
# The `# >>> feature:<name>` / `# <<< feature:<name>` pairs mark passages that
# belong to one optional part of the stack. A variant build that drops that part
# cuts the marked lines out of its own .env.example, so no variant ever ships a
# setting for something it does not contain.

# VITE_OAUTH_CONFIG    — the OIDC discovery URL. The web app reads its OAuth
#                        endpoints from it, AND jwks-fetch derives the JWKS from
#                        it when JWKS_URL is empty. Defaults to the bundled
#                        idp's document: the SPA's default is the RELATIVE
#                        /.well-known/openid-configuration — the browser
#                        resolves it, so discovery follows whatever host the
#                        app is open on, with no edit after attaching a domain.
# VITE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID — the public OAuth client id. Defaults to `public-client`,
#                        the SPA registered in semantius-idp-config/oauth_clients.jsonc.
# VITE_OAUTH_CONFIG must be
# ABSOLUTE (jwks-fetch curls it directly; only the browser can resolve a
# relative one) — and JWKS_URL must be set empty as well, so the keys are
# derived from that issuer's discovery.

# VITE_OAUTH_AUDIENCE — the RFC 8707 resource the SPA asks its access tokens to
# be issued FOR; it becomes their `aud`. The SPA ALWAYS sends one, falling back
# to a built-in placeholder when this is empty, and an issuer that validates
# resources rejects an unknown one — the symptom is a bare "Login Error" on
# /oauth2_callback. Defaults to IDP_JWT_AUDIENCE below (semantius://api), which
# is exactly what the bundled idp registers, so leave it alone unless you are
# replacing the issuer; then set the audience YOUR issuer expects (its issuer
# URL, if it has no separate one).

# VITE_OAUTH_SCOPE — the scopes the SPA asks for. Empty means "whatever the
# issuer's discovery document advertises", which is right for an issuer that
# serves only this stack. Name the API explicitly when the issuer would
# otherwise mint a token for something else: with Entra ID a scope without
# `api://<api-app-id>/<scope>` yields a Microsoft Graph token, which PostgREST
# cannot verify. Include `offline_access`, or every expiry is a full redirect.

# VITE_OAUTH_USERINFO_ENDPOINT — where the SPA reads the signed-in user's name
# and e-mail. Empty means "take it from the discovery document". Set it only
# when that endpoint refuses the token this stack's API is issued for — Entra ID
# advertises Microsoft Graph's, which does exactly that.

# VITE_BACKEND_TYPE / VITE_UI_CUSTOMIZER — which account menu the SPA renders.
# `self_hosted` (the default) links to /idp/account and /idp/admin, the pages
# the bundled idp serves. With an external issuer set `custom` and give
# VITE_UI_CUSTOMIZER a menu — typically one entry pointing at that issuer's own
# profile page, which is then the only place a user can change the name and
# e-mail this stack shows. `Sign out` is rendered by the app and always there.

# --- Everything below has a working default ----------------------------------

# Password for the `postgres` full-DBA login (used by DATABASE_URL / the CLI, and
# by the idp for both its connections). Dev default; change it for anything
# shared or network-exposed. Keep it URL-SAFE — no @ : / ? # or spaces: it is
# spliced into connection URLs for the idp and for pgbouncer, whose entrypoint
# parses them with a naive grep/cut parser.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres

# Database the server creates and migrations deploy into. The idp shares it, in
# its own `idp` schema.
POSTGRES_DB=semantius

# Password for `semantius_authenticator` — the role PostgREST logs in as. It is
# NOSUPERUSER/NOINHERIT and can only SET ROLE authenticated / anon. Read by the
# image's baked 20-authenticator-login.sh AND passed to PostgREST (PGRST_DB_URI) —
# keep the two in sync. Dev default; change it for anything shared or exposed.
# URL-SAFE as well (same reason as POSTGRES_PASSWORD above).
SEMANTIUS_AUTHENTICATOR_PASSWORD=devpassword

# Version tag of the Semantius DB image (ghcr.io/semantius/postgres) to run.
# create/up PULL this tag from GHCR by default; `create.sh --build` builds from
# ./extension and tags :latest locally instead. Pin to a released tag (e.g.
# 0.3.0-pg18) for reproducible/server deploys — released tags always carry the
# -pg<major> suffix; only latest/latest-pg<major> move.
SEMANTIUS_DB_VERSION=latest

# Version tag of the web app image (ghcr.io/semantius/semantius-app) to run.
# It is re-pulled from GHCR on every create (pull_policy: always); pin to a
# released tag for a reproducible/server deploy.
SEMANTIUS_APP_VERSION=latest

# APP_PULL_POLICY / SEMANTIUS_APP_VERSION — how the SPA image is resolved.
# Same mechanism as IDP_PULL_POLICY above: to run an image you built yourself
# (semantius-app's docker/build.sh tags `semantius-app:local`; retag it under
# the ghcr name), point SEMANTIUS_APP_VERSION at that tag AND set
# APP_PULL_POLICY=missing — otherwise the pull replaces your build with the
# registry's copy of the same tag, or fails outright on a tag never pushed.
#   SEMANTIUS_APP_VERSION=local
#   APP_PULL_POLICY=missing

# Optional Northwind demo module. Set to ANY non-empty value (e.g. TRUE) to load
# it on first init; leave unset/empty for an empty database. Only takes effect on
# a FRESH data volume (the image's init scripts run once, at DB creation).
NWIND=TRUE

# PGRST_JWT_ROLE_CLAIM_KEY — which JWT claim names the database role. Default
# `.role`, what the bundled idp mints. An issuer that cannot emit a claim called
# `role` needs the other shape: Entra ID puts an app role named `authenticated`
# into a `roles` ARRAY, so the key becomes `.roles[0]`. pg_semantius reads the
# same array in rbac.uid() when there is no `role` claim, so both ends agree.
# Write the path WITHOUT a leading `$` — compose would interpolate it.

# PGRST_JWT_AUD — the audience PostgREST requires in a token, checked before the
# request reaches the database. Empty means no check at this layer; the database
# still enforces `_settings.jwt_aud` when that row is seeded. Set BOTH for an
# issuer whose signing keys are shared across its tenants (Entra ID is one):
# nothing here validates `iss`, so without an audience check any token that
# issuer ever minted — including one from somebody else's tenant — verifies
# against the JWKS this stack downloaded.

# Host ports. 5432=pgdocker CLI, 5433=pgdocker ext, 5434=this stack, 6432=pgbouncer
# (all side by side).
#
# WEB_PORT is the CADDY FRONT DOOR — the single entry point for the browser and
# the ONLY published HTTP port: `/` the web app, `/rest/*` the PostgREST API,
# `/api-docs/*` the Scalar docs (both prefixes stripped) and `/idp/*` +
# `/.well-known/*` the identity provider (prefix KEPT — it is part of the
# issuer URL). PostgREST, Scalar and the idp have NO host ports of their own:
# every URL the stack emits carries the front door's origin, so the front door
# is the only address that is ever correct to use. WEB_PORT is 3000 — the port
# a browser is conventionally pointed at, and the front door is the only thing
# here a human opens. The data tier keeps its ports: postgres and pgbouncer
# have real non-HTTP clients (psql, your own apps) that caddy cannot front.
POSTGRES_PORT=5434
PGBOUNCER_PORT=6432
WEB_PORT=3000

# SITE_ADDRESS — the address Caddy serves inside the container. The default,
# ":80", is plain HTTP: right for local dev and for anything that terminates TLS
# in front of the stack (Dokploy/Traefik). On a BARE VPS put your bare domain here
# and Caddy obtains a certificate automatically — then publish 80:80 and 443:443
# on the caddy service in docker-compose.yml instead of WEB_PORT. That topology
# answers EVERY Host pointed at the server, so it does NOT meet the conditions
# for IDP_DYNAMIC_ISSUER above — leave that off here.
# SITE_ADDRESS=yourdomain.com

# TRUSTED_PROXIES — the CIDRs whose X-Forwarded-* headers Caddy believes. Only
# the proxy IN FRONT of Caddy belongs here: a trusted caller can forge the
# client address the idp rate-limits and audits by. Unset = 127.0.0.1/32 =
# nobody, right when Caddy is the edge (this file's default). Behind Dokploy's
# Traefik the generated template sets 172.16.0.0/12 (Docker's address pools);
# tighten it to `docker network inspect dokploy-network`'s subnet once known.
# TRUSTED_PROXIES=172.16.0.0/12

# PUBLIC_API_URL — browser-reachable base URL the OpenAPI spec ADVERTISES
# (PGRST_OPENAPI_SERVER_PROXY_URI). PostgREST parses it into the spec's
# host/basePath, so it CANNOT be relative — and it must NEVER be the in-network
# `postgrest` hostname either, because the browser resolves it for every "Test
# Request". Leave unset for local dev (defaults to
# http://localhost:${WEB_PORT}/gateway/rest); going live, set the PUBLIC
# front-door URL INCLUDING that prefix.
#
# The Scalar docs fetch the spec itself from DOCS_SPEC_URL — a SEPARATE setting
# with a RELATIVE default (/gateway/rest): the browser resolves it against
# whatever host the docs are open on, so the docs keep loading on a domain
# attached after deploy, while "Test Request" calls the host advertised by
# PUBLIC_API_URL until you update it. Set DOCS_SPEC_URL only to point the docs
# at a different spec entirely.
#
# /gateway/rest and NOT /rest: both reach the same PostgREST, but /gateway/rest goes
# through the idp's authenticating proxy, which exchanges an `x-api-key` for a
# bearer token — so the docs' "Test Request" works with an API key from
# /idp/account/api-keys instead of a hand-minted JWT. The web app uses the direct
# /rest route (VITE_API_BASE_URL in docker-compose.yml); it already holds an OAuth
# access token and gains nothing from the extra hop.
# PUBLIC_API_URL=https://yourdomain.com/gateway/rest
# DOCS_SPEC_URL=/gateway/rest

# After `docker compose up`, the full-DBA connection string is:
#   DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}@localhost:${POSTGRES_PORT}/${POSTGRES_DB}
#
# The POOLED endpoint (PgBouncer, transaction pooling) for external apps that use
# the SET LOCAL ROLE pattern — pools `semantius_authenticator`, not the DBA login:
#   postgresql://semantius_authenticator:${SEMANTIUS_AUTHENTICATOR_PASSWORD}@localhost:${PGBOUNCER_PORT}/${POSTGRES_DB}
